A new collection edited by Edward Fullbrook and Jamie Morgan, [23 Responses to Economics' Biggest-ever Question](https://rwer.wordpress.com/2026/07/14/23-responses-to-economics-biggest-ever-question/), situates the limits to growth â€” as an intellectual problem, a policy problem, and a disciplinary crisis â€” on the table in terms the economics profession cannot easily dismiss. Subtitled "Economics and the Biophysical Limits to Economic Growth," the volume's framing rests on a claim made by natural science: the global economy caused the climate crisis, and the crisis, now in an accelerating phase, threatens the economy's own existence. For a discipline that has "loosely guided the global economy's national economies" for over a century, that is not a refinement. It is a structural challenge.

## The Policy Architecture Already Built on a Bet

The difficulty is that major institutional decisions have not waited for this reckoning. Green growth â€” the premise that economic expansion can be decoupled from resource use and made consistent with ecological stability â€” is not a fringe hypothesis. [Green growth](https://unevenearth.org/2022/10/green-growth/) by Winne van Woerden for Uneven Earth documents it as the governing assumption embedded in the European Green Deal, the Paris Agreement, IPCC reports, and the US Inflation Reduction Act, promoted by leading multilateral organizations and treated as common sense across national and international policymaking.

That consensus rests on a specific mechanism: decoupling, the idea that economic output can continue to grow while its ecological costs fall. In practice, this has meant reliance on net zero frameworks â€” scenarios in which continued emissions are balanced by future removal of carbon from the atmosphere, predominantly through technological approaches. The promise is that growth does not need to stop; it only needs to clean up after itself, eventually. Van Woerden argues that the empirical basis for that promise is far weaker than the confidence currently invested in it.

The institutional bet embedded in those frameworks is that the answer to the collection's central question is yes â€” growth can continue, cleanly enough, within biophysical limits. If the 23 responses suggest otherwise, the implications don't settle in academic journals. They land in every policy instrument that has already staked its architecture on decoupling working at the required scale.

## The Ecomodernist Premise and Its Discontents

Green growth is not simply an optimistic reading of the data. It rests on a specific intellectual foundation: an ecomodernist belief in technological progress holding that there are no limits to growth because there are no limits to human ingenuity. From this premise, the limits-to-growth tradition represents not a scientific constraint to be accommodated but a failure of imagination to be overcome.

Van Woerden's analysis for Uneven Earth argues that this foundation is dangerously misguided, and that the decoupling concept on which green growth's credibility depends does not hold up to scrutiny. Part of the argument turns on a distinction between relative and absolute decoupling. Relative decoupling â€” where emissions rise more slowly than the economy â€” has been observed in some wealthy nations. But what the green growth framework requires is absolute decoupling: total emissions falling even as the economy expands. The empirical record on this, Van Woerden argues, does not support the confidence placed in it.

A second line of critique concerns what Van Woerden calls the Jevons paradox. Efficiency improvements in how resources are used tend, in growth-oriented economies, to lower costs and stimulate more consumption rather than reduce total resource use. The anticipated gains from greener technologies are absorbed by expanded production and demand, not banked as ecological savings. This means that scaling up clean technology, without addressing the growth dynamic itself, transforms what is intended as an energy transition into an energy expansion in practice.

A third concern is what Van Woerden describes as "carbon tunnel" thinking â€” the tendency of green growth frameworks to focus on reducing atmospheric carbon through technological innovation while treating that as a solution to the broader ecological problem. Earth scientists identify multiple interacting natural systems, not carbon alone, as the relevant boundaries. Policies that assume carbon removal can substitute for addressing the full range of ecological pressures are, on this account, solving a narrower problem than the one that actually exists.

## What the Volume's Arrival Puts on the Table

The Fullbrook and Morgan collection arrived this month. Its framing does not hedge the question: economics has guided the global economy for over a century, the climate crisis that economy produced is now accelerating, and the crisis threatens the economy's own existence. That is the disciplinary problem the volume addresses formally.

Van Woerden's documented case â€” that the decoupling premise underpinning the green growth consensus does not withstand scrutiny â€” maps the contested terrain the collection enters. The economics discipline that has governed global economic decisions for a century is now being asked, by a volume assembling 23 responses to what its editors call economics' biggest-ever question, to reckon with whether the framework guiding that governance has the biophysical foundations it has claimed. The accelerating phase of the crisis the natural sciences describe makes the timeline for that reckoning something the volume's arrival makes newly unavoidable.
